1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,901
Welcome to this complete code walkthrough of One Now
Quick Support.

2
00:00:03,901 --> 00:00:10,285
We will connect what you see on screen to the React
components, the booking rules, local storage, and

3
00:00:10,285 --> 00:00:10,994
the tests.

4
00:00:10,994 --> 00:00:16,669
The problem is simple: a vehicle booked on Turo might
still appear available on another calendar.

5
00:00:16,669 --> 00:00:21,988
This prototype helps an operator report that concern
and helps support explain the next step.

6
00:00:21,988 --> 00:00:24,116
Every booking and connection is simulated.

7
00:00:24,116 --> 00:00:27,663
The app never contacts Turo or repairs a real
calendar.

8
00:00:27,663 --> 00:00:30,500
The narration in this tutorial is computer generated.

9
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,726
Start with the project map.

10
00:00:33,726 --> 00:00:38,349
Index dot HTML supplies a root element and loads main
dot JSX.

11
00:00:38,349 --> 00:00:45,283
Main contains the visible application and imports the
rules from logic dot JS and the design from styles

12
00:00:45,283 --> 00:00:46,053
dot CSS.

13
00:00:46,053 --> 00:00:51,447
Keeping booking rules in separate functions makes
them easier to test without a browser.

14
00:00:51,447 --> 00:00:53,758
Logic dot test verifies those functions.

15
00:00:53,758 --> 00:00:56,840
The browser smoke script exercises real user actions.

16
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:02,618
Package dot JSON lists commands and dependencies,
while the lock file records exact dependency
versions.

17
00:01:02,618 --> 00:01:05,700
There is no server side application or database.

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,523
To run the downloaded project on your computer,
install a supported version of Node, open a terminal
in

19
00:01:13,523 --> 00:01:16,059
the project folder, and run npm ci.

20
00:01:16,059 --> 00:01:19,320
This installs the versions recorded in the lock file.

21
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:25,481
Npm run dev starts Vite, which prints the local
browser address, usually on port fifty one seventy

22
00:01:25,481 --> 00:01:25,843
three.

23
00:01:25,843 --> 00:01:28,017
Npm test runs the unit tests.

24
00:01:28,017 --> 00:01:31,278
Npm run build creates the production files in dist.

25
00:01:31,278 --> 00:01:34,902
Npm run preview serves that production build for
local checking.

26
00:01:34,902 --> 00:01:37,439
The Vite configuration enables the React plugin.

27
00:01:37,439 --> 00:01:40,700
Read the README for the exact Node version
requirements.

28
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,246
React starts by rendering App into the root element.

29
00:01:45,246 --> 00:01:51,018
Inside App, use State remembers tickets, the selected
tab, the form, validation errors, and the selected

30
00:01:51,018 --> 00:01:51,739
ticket identifier.

31
00:01:51,739 --> 00:01:56,789
The active ticket is derived by finding that
identifier in the current tickets array.

32
00:01:56,789 --> 00:02:03,282
This is why an update from support appears in the
operator view: both views read the same application

33
00:02:03,282 --> 00:02:03,643
state.

34
00:02:03,643 --> 00:02:06,889
They are two views of one local data set.

35
00:02:06,889 --> 00:02:11,939
Switching tabs demonstrates the two roles, but it
does not provide authentication or permission

36
00:02:11,939 --> 00:02:12,300
enforcement.

37
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:15,830
The operator form uses controlled inputs.

38
00:02:15,830 --> 00:02:19,917
The value shown in each input comes from the form
object.

39
00:02:19,917 --> 00:02:26,234
When someone types or chooses an option, the change
handler copies the existing form and replaces just

40
00:02:26,234 --> 00:02:26,977
one field.

41
00:02:26,977 --> 00:02:29,950
The spread operator preserves all the other answers.

42
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:34,409
The reusable field helper builds labels, inputs or
selects, and error messages.

43
00:02:34,409 --> 00:02:40,355
Demo buttons copy a predefined scenario into the same
form, rather than using a separate submission

44
00:02:40,355 --> 00:02:40,726
path.

45
00:02:40,726 --> 00:02:45,928
That makes the demonstration reliable while still
exercising the actual validation and ticket creation

46
00:02:45,928 --> 00:02:46,300
code.

47
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:51,259
Validation returns an object whose keys identify
fields with problems.

48
00:02:51,259 --> 00:02:57,480
Required answers are trimmed, so spaces alone do not
count as an operator name or booking reference.

49
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:04,067
The date helper checks the expected year month day
format, parses the date, and compares it with the

50
00:03:04,067 --> 00:03:06,263
normalized result to reject impossible dates.

51
00:03:06,263 --> 00:03:09,922
The end date cannot be earlier than the start date.

52
00:03:09,922 --> 00:03:13,215
Vehicle and connection selections are checked against
allowed choices.

53
00:03:13,215 --> 00:03:19,436
App displays these errors beside the inputs, and
create Ticket validates again so the rule is also

54
00:03:19,436 --> 00:03:20,900
enforced outside the form.

55
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:25,487
The bookings array is a small fictional data set.

56
00:03:25,487 --> 00:03:29,140
Tesla has a Turo reservation and an overlapping
direct reservation.

57
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:32,427
Jeep and Toyota have their own sample Turo
reservations.

58
00:03:32,427 --> 00:03:37,906
The overlaps function asks whether each booking
starts on or before the other booking ends.

59
00:03:37,906 --> 00:03:41,194
If both comparisons are true, their date ranges
overlap.

60
00:03:41,194 --> 00:03:46,673
The dates use a consistent sortable year month day
format, and validation runs before detection.

61
00:03:46,673 --> 00:03:52,517
Shared days count as overlaps because this prototype
does not model pickup and drop off times.

62
00:03:52,517 --> 00:03:56,900
In production, precise time and time zone rules would
need additional design.

63
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:04,624
Detect first looks for a matching sample Turo
reservation and then looks for overlapping records
for the

64
00:04:04,624 --> 00:04:07,269
same vehicle, excluding the reported booking
reference.

65
00:04:07,269 --> 00:04:11,426
A conflict takes highest priority, even when the
connection is disconnected.

66
00:04:11,426 --> 00:04:17,851
If there is no conflict but the connection is
disconnected or unknown, the result is medium
priority.

67
00:04:17,851 --> 00:04:20,118
Otherwise the result is low priority.

68
00:04:20,118 --> 00:04:23,519
Each result contains an explanation and recommended
next steps.

69
00:04:23,519 --> 00:04:28,432
The matched flag separately tells the interface
whether the reported reference was found.

70
00:04:28,432 --> 00:04:34,100
Low priority means no known sample conflict, not
proof that a real calendar is correct.

71
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:38,957
Create Ticket turns the submitted form into a support
record.

72
00:04:38,957 --> 00:04:45,003
It rejects invalid input, trims the form strings,
adds the detection result, and sets the initial
status

73
00:04:45,003 --> 00:04:45,714
to Open.

74
00:04:45,714 --> 00:04:51,405
The identifier is one greater than the largest ticket
number in the current array, formatted with

75
00:04:51,405 --> 00:04:52,116
leading zeroes.

76
00:04:52,116 --> 00:04:57,807
It is convenient for this single browser
demonstration, not a globally unique identifier for a
multi

77
00:04:57,807 --> 00:04:58,519
user service.

78
00:04:58,519 --> 00:05:02,787
The ticket also records a creation time and its first
history entry.

79
00:05:02,787 --> 00:05:05,632
After a full demo reset, numbering starts again.

80
00:05:05,632 --> 00:05:09,900
A real shared service would need server managed
identifiers and concurrency controls.

81
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:15,369
Persistence uses local Storage, which stores strings
in the current browser profile.

82
00:05:15,369 --> 00:05:19,886
JSON stringify converts the tickets array to text;
JSON parse reads it back.

83
00:05:19,886 --> 00:05:23,360
Read Tickets performs a basic structural check on the
result.

84
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:29,961
If saved data is unreadable, the app shows a warning
and prevents creating new tickets over it until the

85
00:05:29,961 --> 00:05:30,656
user resets.

86
00:05:30,656 --> 00:05:37,257
If writing fails, the new ticket state is not
applied, and an error explains that the update was
not

87
00:05:37,257 --> 00:05:37,604
saved.

88
00:05:37,604 --> 00:05:44,205
This is useful error handling, but local Storage is
not a database, a secure vault, or a cross device

89
00:05:44,205 --> 00:05:44,900
synchronization service.

90
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:51,005
The interface has three main component functions:
App, Badge, and Ticket.

91
00:05:51,005 --> 00:05:56,247
Badge creates small status labels using CSS classes
derived from its text.

92
00:05:56,247 --> 00:06:01,053
Ticket displays the report, detection explanation,
matching reference disclosure, conflicting records,

93
00:06:01,053 --> 00:06:04,111
recommended next steps, resolution, and activity
timeline.

94
00:06:04,111 --> 00:06:07,605
Its support prop controls which editing form appears.

95
00:06:07,605 --> 00:06:11,974
Support staff see status and note controls for
unresolved cases.

96
00:06:11,974 --> 00:06:16,342
An operator viewing a resolved case sees the reopen
form.

97
00:06:16,342 --> 00:06:22,021
Because this component is reused, explanations and
updates remain consistent across both views.

98
00:06:22,021 --> 00:06:27,700
The ticket keys separate the local editing state for
each ticket and role.

99
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:34,979
Update Ticket validates the chosen status and rejects
an update that changes nothing and contains no

100
00:06:34,979 --> 00:06:35,353
note.

101
00:06:35,353 --> 00:06:39,837
It returns a new ticket object instead of modifying
the old one.

102
00:06:39,837 --> 00:06:44,321
The history array is also copied, with the new
support event appended.

103
00:06:44,321 --> 00:06:49,926
This immutable style helps React render updates
predictably and makes tests easier to reason about.

104
00:06:49,926 --> 00:06:55,532
App replaces the matching ticket in its array, saves
the array, and shows a confirmation.

105
00:06:55,532 --> 00:07:00,763
Investigating and Waiting for Operator are useful
intermediate statuses: support can record progress
and

106
00:07:00,763 --> 00:07:04,500
requests for information without pretending the
concern is already resolved.

107
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:10,093
A support tool should not close a case without
explaining the outcome.

108
00:07:10,093 --> 00:07:15,459
The resolution guard requires at least twenty trimmed
characters before a ticket can become Resolved.

109
00:07:15,459 --> 00:07:18,321
The interface asks what was verified and
communicated.

110
00:07:18,321 --> 00:07:23,687
The same note is stored as the documented resolution
and added to the activity trail.

111
00:07:23,687 --> 00:07:27,622
This minimum length is a basic guard, not a quality
guarantee.

112
00:07:27,622 --> 00:07:32,988
A human must still confirm the facts and agree on
next steps with the operator.

113
00:07:32,988 --> 00:07:36,207
In a demonstration, clearly describe the outcome as
simulated.

114
00:07:36,207 --> 00:07:40,500
Saving a resolution does not update a calendar or
change a reservation.

115
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:45,478
Reopen Ticket is the final part of the customer
support loop.

116
00:07:45,478 --> 00:07:50,828
It accepts only resolved cases and requires at least
ten trimmed characters describing what is still

117
00:07:50,828 --> 00:07:51,163
happening.

118
00:07:51,163 --> 00:07:56,178
The returned ticket changes back to Open and adds an
operator event to the history.

119
00:07:56,178 --> 00:08:02,197
The old resolution is preserved, and the interface
labels it as a previous resolution while the issue is

120
00:08:02,197 --> 00:08:02,866
open again.

121
00:08:02,866 --> 00:08:06,878
Support can see what was already attempted before
deciding the next action.

122
00:08:06,878 --> 00:08:12,897
The reopening form does not automatically fix or
escalate a real booking; it records a clear request
for

123
00:08:12,897 --> 00:08:13,900
further human investigation.

124
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:21,783
Styles dot CSS supplies the cream background, orange
primary actions, rounded cards, grids, tables, and

125
00:08:21,783 --> 00:08:22,661
priority badges.

126
00:08:22,661 --> 00:08:29,244
Media queries adjust spacing and switch the operator
workspace to one column on small screens.

127
00:08:29,244 --> 00:08:35,828
Focus styles help keyboard users, field errors use
accessibility attributes, and a reduced motion rule

128
00:08:35,828 --> 00:08:37,583
disables transitions when requested.

129
00:08:37,583 --> 00:08:41,972
Reset Demo Data opens a confirmation dialog with
keyboard handling.

130
00:08:41,972 --> 00:08:46,800
Confirming removes the application storage key and
clears local React state.

131
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:48,556
Cancelling keeps the tickets.

132
00:08:48,556 --> 00:08:54,700
These features improve usability, although the
project has not undergone a full accessibility audit.

133
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,347
The thirteen unit tests use Node's built in test
runner.

134
00:09:00,347 --> 00:09:05,564
They check overlapping dates, the three priorities,
unknown connections, validation, ticket numbering,

135
00:09:05,564 --> 00:09:08,607
history, resolution, reopening, and stored data
handling.

136
00:09:08,607 --> 00:09:15,128
The Playwright smoke test launches an isolated
Chromium profile and clicks through the real
interface.

137
00:09:15,128 --> 00:09:20,780
It checks required fields, an urgent ticket,
investigation, the resolution guard, reopening,
reload

138
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:25,127
persistence, other scenarios, mobile overflow, reset,
and corrupt data handling.

139
00:09:25,127 --> 00:09:29,475
The production build was also checked with the
browser script.

140
00:09:29,475 --> 00:09:31,648
These checks passed during implementation.

141
00:09:31,648 --> 00:09:37,300
They do not prove every browser works; Firefox and
Safari were not tested.

142
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:41,823
For your assessment recording, tell a simple support
story.

143
00:09:41,823 --> 00:09:44,689
Load the urgent demo and submit the report.

144
00:09:44,689 --> 00:09:50,419
Explain that the sample direct reservation overlaps
the Turo booking, so a human should review it

145
00:09:50,419 --> 00:09:50,778
immediately.

146
00:09:50,778 --> 00:09:54,717
Move to the dashboard, record an investigation note,
and save it.

147
00:09:54,717 --> 00:09:58,299
Then document a clearly simulated outcome and resolve
the case.

148
00:09:58,299 --> 00:10:02,597
Return to the operator view and show the shared
timeline and resolution.

149
00:10:02,597 --> 00:10:06,179
Finish by explaining how the operator can reopen the
ticket.

150
00:10:06,179 --> 00:10:12,267
The README has a timed sequence for a separate
walkthrough under three minutes; this longer tutorial
is

151
00:10:12,267 --> 00:10:13,700
for understanding the code.

152
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,670
You now have the full code map.

153
00:10:17,670 --> 00:10:23,390
React handles the views and state, pure functions
handle booking and ticket rules, local Storage

154
00:10:23,390 --> 00:10:26,823
preserves the demo, and tests verify the important
paths.

155
00:10:26,823 --> 00:10:32,543
If this became a real product, it would need
authenticated roles, a shared backend, trustworthy

156
00:10:32,543 --> 00:10:35,975
integration data, stronger data validation, and
proper privacy controls.

157
00:10:35,975 --> 00:10:37,882
Those are outside this prototype.

158
00:10:37,882 --> 00:10:43,603
For this assessment, the most important design choice
is the complete support loop: explain uncertainty,

159
00:10:43,603 --> 00:10:50,086
escalate booking conflicts, document the outcome, and
let the operator tell you when the issue is still

160
00:10:50,086 --> 00:10:50,468
unresolved.

161
00:10:50,468 --> 00:10:53,900
The source and README are in your GitHub repository.
